---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'three', label: 'Supported screen readers' },
   { id: 'virtual', label: 'What the simulated screen reader is' },
   { id: 'choose', label: 'Which screen reader to use' },
   { id: 'default', label: 'Which screen reader runs by default' },
   { id: 'setup', label: 'Set up VoiceOver or NVDA' },
   { id: 'label', label: 'How results are labeled' },
   { id: 'talkback', label: 'TalkBack on Android' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Screen readers"
   summary="<p>a11ied drives three screen readers with the same commands: VoiceOver, NVDA, and a simulated one that runs anywhere. This page explains what each is good for and what a result from each one proves.</p>"
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section>
      <h2 id="three">Supported screen readers</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
         <table>
            <thead>
               <tr><th scope="col"><code>--sr</code></th><th scope="col">What it is</th><th scope="col">Runs on</th><th scope="col">Setup</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
               <tr><th scope="row"><code>virtual</code></th><td>A simulation of a screen reader</td><td>macOS, Windows, Linux, CI</td><td>None</td></tr>
               <tr><th scope="row"><code>voiceover</code></th><td>Apple's VoiceOver</td><td>macOS</td><td><code>a1 setup</code>, once</td></tr>
               <tr><th scope="row"><code>nvda</code></th><td>NVDA, the free screen reader for Windows</td><td>Windows</td><td><code>a1 setup</code>, once</td></tr>
            </tbody>
         </table>
      </div>
      <p>
         a11ied drives VoiceOver and NVDA through
         <a href="https://www.guidepup.dev/">Guidepup</a>, an open-source library.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="virtual">What the simulated screen reader is</h2>
      <p>
         The simulated screen reader is software that reads the page's accessibility tree,
         the structure a browser gives to assistive technology, and works out what a screen
         reader would announce for each element. It makes no sound and takes over nothing.
      </p>
      <p>It runs in one of two engines, and tells you which when a session starts.</p>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>browser</code></dt>
         <dd>A headless Chromium page. Page scripts run, so dialogs open and live regions announce. This is what you get for a URL.</dd>
         <dt><code>jsdom</code></dt>
         <dd>An in-memory document with no browser. Page scripts do not run, so only what is in the markup is announced. This is what you get for inline HTML in a test, or when no Chromium browser is installed.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         It is a model, and a real screen reader differs from it. VoiceOver and NVDA word
         things differently from the model and from each other, and each has bugs and
         shortcuts the model does not have. A transcript from the simulated screen reader
         shows what the page exposes. It does not show what VoiceOver or NVDA says.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="choose">Which screen reader to use</h2>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt>Simulated</dt>
         <dd>While you build. In tests. In CI. For an AI agent. It is fast, it gives the same result on every machine, and tests can run many at once.</dd>
         <dt>VoiceOver or NVDA</dt>
         <dd>Before a release. To reproduce a user's bug report. To record evidence for an audit. Whenever the question is what a real user hears.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         A good habit: develop against the simulated screen reader, then confirm the
         behavior that matters once with a real one.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="default">Which screen reader runs by default</h2>
      <p>
         Without <code>--sr</code>, <code>a1 sr start</code> uses the screen reader of the
         platform when it is set up: VoiceOver on macOS, NVDA on Windows. Otherwise it uses
         the simulated one and prints a warning that says so. On Linux it is always the
         simulated one.
      </p>
      <p>
         A real screen reader takes over the keyboard and speaks. If that is not what you
         want, pass <code>--sr virtual</code>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="setup">Set up VoiceOver or NVDA</h2>
      <pre><code>a1 setup
a1 doctor --task reader</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>a1 setup</code> runs Guidepup's setup. On macOS it lets VoiceOver be controlled
         by scripts. On Windows it installs a copy of NVDA for automation. <code>a1 doctor</code>
         then shows what is ready, with the command for anything that is not.
      </p>
      <p>
         To record the screen during a session, the app you run <code>a1</code> from also
         needs permission. See <a href="/guides/recording">record a screen reader session</a>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="label">How results are labeled</h2>
      <p>Every session and every JSON result says where it came from.</p>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>target</code></dt>
         <dd><code>virtual</code>, <code>voiceover</code>, or <code>nvda</code>.</dd>
         <dt><code>targetType</code></dt>
         <dd><code>simulated</code> or <code>real</code>.</dd>
         <dt><code>engine</code></dt>
         <dd>For the simulated screen reader, <code>browser</code> or <code>jsdom</code>.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>Keep these with any transcript you put in a report.</p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="talkback">TalkBack on Android</h2>
      <p>
         TalkBack is not an <code>--sr</code> choice. <a href="/reference/cli#talkback"><code>a1
         talkback</code></a> turns it on or off on a phone or emulator and prints what it
         says as focus moves.
      </p>
      <p>
         For speech recorded from VoiceOver, TalkBack, NVDA, and JAWS on specific ARIA
         features, see <a href="/compatibility">screen reader compatibility</a>.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
